1
00:00:00,800 --> 00:00:07,000
I was born and raised in Harar. All my childhood memories began there.

2
00:00:07,600 --> 00:00:15,000
About 2.5 kilometers from my childhood home stood the Ras Makonnen Monument at a large roundabout.

3
00:00:15,500 --> 00:00:23,500
As a child, passing that monument often meant my parents were buying me new clothes or taking me somewhere special.

4
00:00:24,100 --> 00:00:31,000
Those were unforgettable family outings. Seeing the monument always filled me with happiness and excitement.

5
00:00:31,800 --> 00:00:37,500
Years passed, and I grew up and left Harar to attend university.

6
00:00:38,100 --> 00:00:45,000
I spent around five years in Adama and Addis Ababa, continuing my studies far from my hometown.

7
00:00:45,600 --> 00:00:52,500
When I returned to Harar years later, I expected to see all the familiar places again.

8
00:00:53,100 --> 00:01:01,500
But when I saw that the monument had been demolished, I felt deeply disappointed and sad. A familiar part of my childhood memories was gone.

9
00:01:02,000 --> 00:01:11,500
Even though I was back in my hometown, Harar felt different from the place I remembered. Familiar places hold our memories and become part of who we are.

10
00:01:12,100 --> 00:01:20,000
Heritage is not only about physical monuments; it is also about the memories and emotions we attach to places.
